2. Premise Laundering

From “This Could Easily Be Wrong” to “Proves Beyond Doubt”

Amodei repeatedly acknowledges uncertainty.

In Machines of Loving Grace, he says his predictions could easily be wrong, that he expects errors outside his areas of expertise and that his account should be treated as a starting prompt for a better-informed group of specialists. His estimate that AI will compress fifty to one hundred years of biological progress into five to ten years is described as a guess; the later list of consequences is explicitly “not based on any rigorous methodology.” 

This is admirable candour at the beginning of a speculative essay.

It becomes less admirable when the same speculative structure is later used as the basis for urgent institutional authority.

In Policy on the AI Exponential, Amodei writes that recent evidence of AI power and risk has become “undeniable,” that Anthropic’s Claude Mythos Preview “scrambled” the cybersecurity landscape and that this “proves beyond doubt” that AI models are now instruments of national and global strategic consequence. From there he predicts that biological and autonomy risks may soon follow and proposes mandatory testing, authorised evaluators and government power to block deployment. 

Notice what has happened.

The argument has moved through four epistemic states:

Essay 1:
Let us assume powerful AI for the sake of discussion.
Essay 2:
I increasingly believe it is likely.
Essay 3:
The evidence is undeniable.
Essay 4:
Government must possess emergency powers to stop deployment.

No comparably dramatic evidential bridge is supplied between those states.

Anthropic’s own model and Anthropic’s own risk research may be relevant evidence. They cannot, by themselves, establish every downstream proposition Anthropic’s chief executive attaches to them. Demonstrating improved cyber capability does not prove an imminent autonomous civilisation, mass biological destruction, permanent totalitarianism or economy-wide human redundancy.

The hypothesis walks into the first essay wearing slippers.

It leaves the final essay in an FAA uniform carrying statutory authority.

