3. Machines of Loving Grace

A Century of Medicine Produced by Multiplying Intelligence by Ten

Amodei predicts that powerful AI could increase the rate of biological discovery by at least tenfold, delivering fifty to one hundred years of progress in five to ten years. He says he is open to the possibility of obtaining one thousand years of progress in the same period, although he regards one hundred years in a single year as implausible. 

From that estimate follow:

* prevention or treatment of almost all natural infectious disease;
* elimination of most cancer, potentially reducing incidence and mortality by 95% or more;
* prevention or cure of most genetic disease;
* prevention or cure of most mental illness;
* substantial improvement of ordinary human emotional experience;
* and a possible doubling of human lifespan to approximately 150 years. 

The estimate is not derived from a model of biological discovery.

It is derived from the intuition that a vast number of very intelligent workers would advance discovery much faster.

That is not nothing. Intelligence is unquestionably relevant to scientific work.

But scientific progress is not:

\text{Progress} =
\text{number of clever thoughts}
\times
\text{thinking speed}

It also depends on:

\begin{aligned}
&\text{physical experiments} \\
+&\text{measurement accuracy} \\
+&\text{biological variation} \\
+&\text{manufacturing capability} \\
+&\text{instrumentation} \\
+&\text{materials} \\
+&\text{clinical recruitment} \\
+&\text{long-term observation} \\
+&\text{rare adverse events} \\
+&\text{distribution and access}
\end{aligned}

Amodei recognises some of these constraints, but then treats larger effect sizes, better simulations and massive parallelism as mechanisms by which many of them will shrink.

That is a possibility.

It is not a quantified forecast.

The FDA explains that large later-stage trials are used not merely because regulators enjoy paperwork, but because uncommon side effects can remain invisible in smaller studies and because clinical evidence must establish real treatment benefit in a population. FDA’s current position is that AI may improve drug development, while still requiring context-specific evaluation of reliability, explainability, safety and generalisability. 

This creates a particularly awkward contradiction across Amodei’s writings.

In The Urgency of Interpretability, he says current AI systems are opaque, that we cannot precisely explain why they make particular decisions and that relying on vastly more capable systems without understanding them would be “basically unacceptable.”

In Policy on the AI Exponential, he suggests that AI simulation and analysis may replace parts of pharmacology, toxicology and clinical testing. 

So the proposed evidential chain becomes:

We do not understand how the AI reaches its conclusions.
The AI predicts that an intervention is safe.
The prediction may reduce the amount of physical testing required.
The resulting reduction proves that AI accelerates medicine.

An opaque model does not become a validated substitute for biological evidence because the chief executive has placed it inside a hopeful essay.

The phrase “compressed 21st century” is not a methodology.

It is a trailer voice.

