4. On DeepSeek and Export Controls

A Policy That Wins Regardless of What the Evidence Does

After DeepSeek demonstrated strong performance with lower reported training costs, Amodei argued that the result did not weaken the case for US export controls on advanced chips. He said it made those controls even more existentially important. 

There may be legitimate national-security reasons for restricting advanced semiconductor exports. That is a real policy question.

Amodei’s argumentative structure, however, is beautifully self-sealing:

China falls behind:
Export controls are working.
China catches up:
Export controls are more urgently needed.
China becomes more efficient:
Efficiency makes each chip more dangerous, so controls must tighten.
China obtains restricted chips:
That proves enforcement must expand.
China uses unrestricted chips:
Those chips should also be restricted.

No observable outcome appears capable of weakening the thesis.

That does not make the thesis false.

It makes the presentation unfalsifiable.

Amodei additionally inferred that some reported H100s in DeepSeek’s fleet were likely smuggled, while acknowledging that the precise external picture could not be fully determined. He used reported fleet composition and secondary estimates to support a broad national-security policy from which major US frontier laboratories would foreseeably benefit. 

Anthropic is not a neutral state-security institute. It is a US AI company valued at $965 billion, backed by enormous amounts of private capital and competing with Chinese laboratories for customers, talent, investment and geopolitical position. 

That conflict does not settle the export-control argument.

But it must be included in it.

A chief executive arguing that foreign competitors should be denied essential inputs may be correct about national security. He is still a chief executive arguing that foreign competitors should be denied essential inputs.

The economic interest does not vanish because the article mentions democracy.

