5. The Urgency of Interpretability

We Must Understand the Machine—Shortly After We Finish Deploying It

This is probably the cleanest internal contradiction in the entire corpus.

Amodei says:

* modern generative systems are fundamentally opaque;
* we do not know precisely why they produce particular decisions;
* opacity prevents reliable prediction of dangerous behaviour;
* there is no solid real-world evidence of power-seeking or deception;
* current interpretability work exposes only a small fraction of likely features and circuits;
* a mature “MRI for AI” may arrive within five to ten years;
* but systems equivalent to a country of geniuses may arrive as soon as 2026 or 2027. 

Define:

T_G = \text{arrival of the country of geniuses}

T_I = \text{arrival of reliable comprehensive interpretability}

Amodei’s own estimates imply:

T_G \approx 2026\text{–}2027

T_I \approx 2030\text{–}2035

Therefore:

T_G < T_I

He then says deploying the former without a better handle on the latter would be essentially unacceptable.

Yet Anthropic continues to train and release increasingly capable models under its Responsible Scaling Policy, which Anthropic itself describes as a voluntary framework. 

The practical policy is consequently not:

Do not deploy systems we cannot understand.

It is:

Continue deploying while our company decides whether our partial understanding and safeguards are sufficient.

That is a very different standard.

It leaves the manufacturer as:

* the builder;
* the evaluator;
* the threshold designer;
* the risk narrator;
* the commercial seller;
* and one of the primary advisers proposing how the government should regulate the product.

The “MRI” metaphor also does more rhetorical work than the research presently supports. Amodei concedes that only a fraction of likely features have been identified, that only a small number of circuits have been manually traced and that a major gap remains between finding features and diagnosing practical risks. 

A sparse feature map is not an MRI of cognition.

A circuit associated with an output is not a complete causal explanation of a model.

And behaviour consistent with deception is not proof of an internal enduring desire to deceive.

Amodei himself admits that there is no solid real-world evidence of model power-seeking. Yet the essay repeatedly speaks of catching models “red-handed” thinking deceitful or power-hungry thoughts. 

That language collapses three different propositions:

\text{Output resembles deception}

\text{Mechanism optimises a deceptive strategy}

\text{Entity possesses an intention to deceive}

Those are not interchangeable.

The personification is not harmless decoration. It prepares the reader to accept political claims about machines possessing motives before the scientific work has established what kind of mechanism is actually present.

First the model is grown like bacteria.

Then it becomes a thinker.

Then a liar.

Then a sovereign threat.

Then Congress receives the invoice for the scanner.

