The essay says stopping or substantially slowing AI is “fundamentally untenable.” Amodei writes that the formula is so simple that advanced AI almost emerges spontaneously from data and computation, and that its creation may have become inevitable when humanity invented the transistor—or perhaps when it learned to control fire. 

This is not physics.

No conservation law requires Anthropic to raise another funding round.

No theorem compels Google and Broadcom to provide it with multiple gigawatts of TPU capacity.

No property of fire requires Amazon to invest billions. 

“Inexorable” is useful corporate grammar because it converts deliberate choices into weather.

Anthropic does not merely observe an external technological current. It recruits researchers, raises capital, purchases compute, builds products, signs government contracts and accelerates the systems Amodei describes.

The company is not trapped on the bus.

It is building the engine while explaining that nobody can reach the brake.

The complete decision tree is:

If AI is safe:
Build it for the benefits.
If AI is dangerous:
Build it before autocracies do.
If AI is uncertain:
Build it to obtain more evidence.
If AI is opaque:
Build it while developing interpretability.
If AI destroys jobs:
Build it and design redistribution afterward.
If society objects:
The technology is inevitable anyway.

Every branch returns the same output:

\boxed{\text{Continue building}}

That is not a dilemma.

That is a constant disguised as one.

