7.1 Maximum Caution for AI, Reduced Caution for AI-Generated Medicine

Amodei argues that frontier AI should face binding government testing because its risks are becoming undeniable.

In the same essay, he argues that medical regulators may need to reduce testing delays, accept AI-based simulations, use synthetic control arms and avoid “excessive skepticism” toward interventions that appear to work unexpectedly well. 

This creates a remarkable asymmetry:

When evaluating the AI model:
Treat hypothetical dangers with exceptional caution.
When evaluating products proposed by the AI model:
Prepare to reduce physical testing and regulatory skepticism.

But these are the same opaque systems.

If their internal reasoning is insufficiently understood to rule out deception, jailbreaks or dangerous capabilities, their medical predictions cannot simultaneously inherit privileged evidential status without independent validation.

“AI produced it” cannot be both a reason for extraordinary suspicion and a reason to shorten the route to human use.

The safety posture changes according to which direction moves the AI economy forward.

