8. Open Weights

Public Good Until the Provider Declares the Threshold Crossed

Amodei’s latest statement says Anthropic has never advocated a categorical ban on open-weight models. It calls non-dangerous open weights a public good but argues that highly capable open systems can present greater risks because safeguards and monitoring are difficult and released weights cannot be recalled. It recommends testing sufficiently capable open and closed models rather than blanket prohibition. 

That position is more nuanced than “ban open source.”

It also relocates the entire political problem into two questions:

1. What counts as dangerous capability?
2. Who gets to decide?

If the threshold is shaped by tests, assumptions and threat models developed predominantly by incumbent closed-model laboratories, then openness survives only below the capability boundary those incumbents help define.

The resulting market could look like:

Small models:
Open and publicly beneficial.
Frontier models:
Too dangerous to release.
Frontier capability:
Retained by companies able to satisfy expensive security
and regulatory requirements.
Determination of frontier danger:
Strongly influenced by those same companies.

That may still be preferable to unrestricted release of genuinely dangerous systems.

But it is not merely safety policy.

It is also a market structure.

The company operating the closed frontier should not be the only institution explaining where public openness must end.

