9.3 The Safety Company and the Pirate Library

In July 2026, a federal judge approved Anthropic’s $1.5 billion settlement of a class action concerning books obtained from pirate repositories. An earlier ruling distinguished between training on lawfully acquired books—which the judge held was fair use in that case—and Anthropic’s acquisition and retention of pirated copies, which remained exposed to liability. The settlement resolves those piracy claims without converting every disputed AI-training practice into infringement. 

That distinction matters.

So does the event.

A company that presents itself as unusually serious about responsible development built part of its data estate using hundreds of thousands of unlawfully sourced books and then agreed to the largest known US copyright settlement.

This does not prove that Anthropic is uniquely wicked.

It proves that the label safety company is not a moral exemption certificate.

Safety did not prevent the commercial organisation from taking a shortcut where data acquisition was concerned.

External law, litigation and rightsholders did.

The broader lesson is precisely the opposite of the company’s implicit authority claim:

Institutions should not be trusted merely because they have written elegant documents explaining their own virtue.

