10. Wealth Is Not Epistemology

Dario Amodei is technically accomplished. His official biography records senior research roles at Google Brain and OpenAI, work on major language models, biophysics training and involvement in reinforcement learning from human feedback. 

That gives him relevant expertise.

It does not grant comprehensive authority over:

* macroeconomics;
* employment;
* clinical-trial design;
* constitutional law;
* warfare;
* international relations;
* democratic theory;
* human meaning;
* mental health;
* development economics;
* or the moral destiny of the species.

Expertise is local.

Corporate essays tend to make it imperial.

The argument is not:

Dario is wealthy, therefore wrong.

It is:

\text{Commercial success} \neq \text{scientific validation}

\text{Technical expertise} \neq \text{universal policy competence}

\text{Company mission} \neq \text{democratic mandate}

A $965 billion valuation proves that investors expect Anthropic to capture enormous economic value.

It does not prove that the country of geniuses exists.

It does not prove that its arrival date is correct.

And it certainly does not prove that Anthropic’s chief executive is the best person to allocate the political powers supposedly required to govern it.

